            <ArticleTitle>Incidence and risk factors for symptoms of decompression sickness among male and female dive masters and instructors--a retrospective cohort study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>93-102</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">The aim was to determine the incidence of symptoms of decompression sickness (DCS) in dive masters and instructors in relation to number of dives and possible risk factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective cohort study of dive masters and instructors in Sweden. STUDY BASE: All dive masters and instructors listed with PADI, NAUI and CMAS in Sweden as of January 1st 1999 (2380 divers).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The dive masters and instructors received a validated questionnaire on diving activities and symptoms of DCS in 1999. 1516 men and 226 women answered, i.e. 73% of the initial study base.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">DCS symptoms were reported by 190 divers. The incidence of DCS symptoms was 1.52 for males and 1.27 for females per 1000 dives. Dive masters, divers not performing decompression-stop dives, divers not practicing advanced diving and divers with a low number of total lifetime dives had a higher proportion (p &lt; 0.05) of DCS symptoms per 1000 dives. There were no major differences in DCS symptom incidence related to sex, age, asthma, overweight or alcohol abuse in this study.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
